export default "# Abdul Habeeb\n\nData Science and Full-Stack Developer, 25, based in Saarbrücken, Germany. Pronouns: he/him.\nMaster's student in Data Science and AI at Saarland University (Universität des Saarlandes), 2024 to now.\nBachelor's in Computer Science, Visvesvaraya Technological University, India, 2019 to 2023. Grade: CGPA 1.4 converted to the German scale.\n\nContact: abdulhabeeb9731@gmail.com · https://abdulhabeeb.com/#contact · https://www.linkedin.com/in/abdulhabeeb9731/ · https://github.com/Habeeb9731\nResume (PDF): https://abdulhabeeb.com/public/resources/Abdul_Habeeb_Resume.pdf\n\n## Summary\n\nData Science and Full-Stack Developer with experience building responsive web applications and data-driven solutions. Works across Python, SQL, JavaScript, React, Vue.js, Node.js, FastAPI, PostgreSQL, data analysis, data visualization and machine learning fundamentals. Likes turning complex systems and datasets into tools people can understand and use with confidence.\n\n## Experience\n\n### FGVT Research Group (Forschungsgruppe Verkehrstelematik), htw saar — Research Assistant, Saarbrücken, 2025 to now\n- Analyses research and transportation data for FGVT projects and evidence-based decisions.\n- Prepares, cleans and structures datasets for statistical analysis and machine learning workflows.\n- Builds data visualizations and analytical reports for research partners.\n\n### Moltomedia GmbH — Full-Stack Developer (Werkstudent), Saarbrücken, 2025 to 2026\n- Built responsive web applications with product managers and developers, aligned with user and business needs.\n- Developed reusable frontend components, integrated REST APIs and implemented backend functionality for interactive workflows.\n- Connected frontend interfaces with backend services and structured data; documented API integrations, components and implementation guidelines.\n\n### Zephyr Technologies — Front-End Developer, India, 2023 to 2024\n- Developed and maintained full-stack client web applications with JavaScript, React, REST APIs and backend services.\n- Implemented frontend interfaces, server-side functionality and database integrations.\n- Optimized applications for performance, accessibility, security and cross-browser compatibility.\n\n## Skills and tools\n\n- Frontend: React, Vue.js, JavaScript, TypeScript, Next.js, responsive UI\n- Backend: Node.js, REST APIs, FastAPI\n- Data: Python, pandas, Jupyter, SQL, PostgreSQL, MySQL, DuckDB, data modeling, data cleaning, data analysis, data visualization\n- Machine learning: LightGBM, recommender systems, forecasting, evaluation and backtesting\n- Delivery: Git workflows, Docker, Cloudflare deployments, Hetzner servers, systemd, GitHub Actions, maintainable handovers\n- Communication: analytical reports, reusable documentation, cross-team collaboration\n\n## Awards\n\n- 2026: First place, most creative project, a web-based escape-room experience.\n- 2025: First place, Estonia hackathon, building a sustainable green-development process with an international team.\n- 2025: Second place, Germany hackathon, a university flashcard app inspired by Anki.\n\n## Workshops (Erasmus)\n\n- Web Development Workshop, St. Etienne, France\n- GreenTech Data Challenge, Tallinn, Estonia\n- AI and Data Visualization Workshop, Kaunas, Lithuania\n\n## Volunteering\n\n- ESN Estonia — Communication Manager, 2026\n- Google Developer Student Club — Web Lead, India, 2021 to 2023\n\n## Client words\n\n- Sana, CEO of Ecliniqs (speech therapy, Dubai): \"Habeeb turned our speech therapy services into a website that feels calm and clear for the families we work with.\"\n- Afran, CEO of GrandLense (wedding photography): \"Our portfolio finally looks as polished as the weddings we shoot. He understood the mood we wanted from the first call.\"\n- Ashif, CEO of Modern Luxury Interiors (Dubai): \"He captured the luxury finish of our interiors and delivered a site our clients compliment us on.\"\n- Ansar, CEO of Chick Bites Shawarma: \"Quick, responsive, and easy to work with. Our menu and story now look great on every phone.\"\n\n## Blog\n\nHe runs a blog at https://abdulhabeeb.com/blog/ with daily AI and data news, written automatically by Claude Haiku from RSS feeds, with free Pixabay covers, on a pipeline he built that runs on his own Hetzner server.\n\n## Projects\n\n### depower (Data · Energy forecasting)\nA daily forecast of German day-ahead electricity prices, locked in before the auction closes and scored in public once the real prices arrive.\nLinks: live: https://depower.abdulhabeeb.com/ · repo: https://github.com/Habeeb9731/german-day-ahead-forecast\nRole: Data science & full-stack development\nTimeline: 2026\nType: Personal project\nStack: Python, LightGBM, Next.js\nGermany’s wholesale electricity prices for each hour of tomorrow are set in an auction at 12:00 today. Every morning before that, depower forecasts all 24 prices with a likely range for each hour, using grid data from SMARD and ECMWF weather forecasts. Each forecast is logged once and can’t be overwritten. When the real prices are published, it gets scored in public. Live forecasts began on 3 November 2026, for delivery the next day.\nChallenge: A forecast is only honest if it uses what was known at the time\n- One value published after the auction is enough to make a backtest look better than the model could ever be live.\n- SMARD keeps no history of when values were published, and some forecast series only seem to appear after the auction closes.\n- A good error score proves little on its own. It needs simple baselines to beat, and a clear line between simulated and real results.\nSolution: Make leaking hard, then publish the misses\n- Every cleaned row carries an available-at time, and features can only read through a view that hides anything newer than the 11:30 cutoff.\n- Tests overwrite post-cutoff values with absurd numbers and confirm no feature changes. An audit of all 23,136 real feature rows found none past its cutoff.\n- Each log entry stores the hash of the one before and CI fails if a forecast changes. The dashboard shows backtest and live results separately.\nFeatures: Tomorrow’s prices: 24 hourly forecasts with P10–P90 ranges and the cheapest and priciest windows, as bars, ranges or a table.; Public track record: Forecasts scored against published prices and two simple baselines, with live and backtest results kept apart.; EV charging calculator: Finds the cheapest hours to charge within your plug-in window and estimates the saving, on wholesale prices only.\nData: SMARD (Bundesnetzagentur) for prices, demand and generation, Open-Meteo for ECMWF weather at six locations, and an offline holidays package.\nModel: LightGBM quantile regression for P10, P50 and P90 on 72 features, retrained monthly in the backtest, with conformal calibration by hour.\nPipeline: Python and DuckDB. Raw responses are versioned, quality checks gate every forecast, and a late or incomplete run publishes nothing.\nFrontend: Next.js static export with shadcn/ui and Recharts, in English and German.\nDeployment: Docker containers on a Hetzner server, run by systemd timers at 10:20, 10:45 and 11:10 Berlin time. GitHub Actions runs CI.\nStack: Python, LightGBM, DuckDB, pandas, TypeScript, Next.js, Recharts, shadcn/ui, Docker, systemd, Hetzner, GitHub Actions\nWhat he learned: My first backtest’s P10–P90 ranges covered only 58.5% of real prices when they should have covered 80%. Calibration brought that to 77.9%, but I chose that fix after seeing the result, so the number may be slightly flattering, and the project says so. That taught me a forecast earns trust less through its error score than by showing what it could see, where it fails and what a simple baseline would have done.\nCase study: https://abdulhabeeb.com/projects/depower/\n\n### APEX F1 Analytics (Data · F1 analytics)\nA historical Formula 1 dashboard built on OpenF1 data — races, telemetry, tire strategy and championships, with an AI race analyst on top.\nLinks: live: https://apex.abdulhabeeb.com/ · repo: https://github.com/Habeeb9731/apex-f1-analytics\nRole: Design & full-stack development\nTimeline: 2026\nType: Personal project\nStack: TypeScript, React, Node.js\nOpenF1 publishes timing, positions, laps, stints and telemetry for every session since 2023 — but as raw API responses. APEX is a dashboard that turns that into season overviews, race replays, head-to-head driver comparisons, tire strategy charts and championship tables, with no account or paid API needed.\nChallenge: Raw, heavy and inconsistent data\n- Telemetry for a single session can run to hundreds of thousands of points per driver.\n- Standings, positions and weather arrive as separate endpoints with gaps and missing snapshots.\n- An AI assistant needed to be useful without exposing API keys or running up costs.\nSolution: Fetch less, cache more, explain clearly\n- Telemetry loads two drivers for a five-minute window at a time, filtered at the API and downsampled before charting.\n- Requests are cached and cancelled when abandoned (TanStack Query); selections live in the URL so views are shareable.\n- Ask Apex runs server-side behind GitHub sign-in, with a daily per-user question limit stored in SQLite.\nFeatures: Race explorer: Session classification, position changes and lap-by-lap progress for every Grand Prix.; Driver comparison: Head-to-head lap times and performance metrics for any two drivers.; Ask Apex: An AI race analyst that answers questions about the data — key kept server-side, usage rate-limited.\nData: OpenF1 historical API (2023 onward) — meetings, sessions, laps, stints, positions, telemetry, weather.\nFrontend: React + TypeScript on Vite, TanStack Query for caching and cancellation.\nBackend: Node server for GitHub OAuth and the AI analyst; usage counts in SQLite.\nDeployment: Docker, served through a Cloudflare Tunnel at apex.abdulhabeeb.com.\nStack: TypeScript, React, Vite, TanStack Query, Node.js, SQLite, OpenAI API, GitHub OAuth, Docker, Cloudflare\nWhat he learned: Working with real sports data is mostly about deciding what not to load. Designing around windowed, downsampled requests made the dashboard fast — and taught me to treat API limits as a design constraint, not an afterthought.\nCase study: https://abdulhabeeb.com/projects/apex-f1-analytics/\n\n### AnimeRec (Machine learning · Data)\nA hybrid anime recommender built on 23.2M real list interactions. It handles brand-new visitors and titles nobody has rated yet, and was tested on a held-out time window.\nLinks: live: https://anime.abdulhabeeb.com/ · repo: https://github.com/Habeeb9731/anime-recommender\nRole: Data science & ML engineering\nTimeline: 2026\nType: Personal project\nStack: Python, EASE, Streamlit\nAnimeRec ranks every eligible title for each visitor. It leans on what similar viewers enjoyed, then shifts smoothly toward synopsis and metadata signals as a title’s history falls to zero. Answer three quick questions and your top 10 appears instantly, with no retraining and a reason attached to each pick.\nChallenge: An evaluation that could be trusted\n- Collaborative filtering cannot rank a title nobody has watched yet.\n- The newer dataset looked ideal, but 97.0% of 9.8M sampled “interaction dates” were really air dates or placeholders.\n- Anime viewing follows the airing season, so a plain popularity baseline makes any model look better than it is.\nSolution: Real timestamps, a harder baseline\n- Switched to a crawl with per-user update times, then joined synopses from the newer dataset by anime ID.\n- Split globally by time and ranked the full 10,720-title catalog, with no negative sampling and the test window scored only once.\n- Each title blends collaborative and content scores by how much history it has, and results are reported against recent popularity too.\nFeatures: Instant taste profile: Pick genres and a few favourites, and new users are folded into the model on the spot, without retraining.; A reason for every pick: Recommendations say why they are there, like “Because you liked One Punch Man”, and every title opens a detail card.; Room for new titles: A title with no viewing history can still be scored from its synopsis, genres, studio, type and year.\nData: 23.2M interactions from 100,000 sampled users, after logged filters removed bulk imports and bot-like accounts.\nModels: EASE and ALS for collaborative filtering, and all-MiniLM-L6-v2 sentence embeddings with metadata for content.\nHybrid: Per title, alpha = n / (n + κ) moves the score from collaborative to content signals as its history n falls to zero.\nDemo: A Streamlit app in Docker behind a Cloudflare Tunnel, serving the same model that was evaluated.\nStack: Python, NumPy, SciPy, Polars, implicit, Sentence Transformers, Streamlit, Docker, Cloudflare Tunnel\nWhat he learned: The most useful step came before any model: auditing the timestamps. It stopped me from reporting a time split that was not really one. It also taught me to publish the less flattering numbers. The hybrid only matches plain EASE overall, and new titles rarely reach a top 10.\nCase study: https://abdulhabeeb.com/projects/anime-recommender/\n\n### Autonomous Data Analyst (Data · Full-stack)\nAn AI-assisted analytics workspace: upload a dataset and get a data-quality report, findings and charts — backed by a deterministic profiling engine.\nLinks: live: https://autonomous-data-analyst.abdulhabeeb.com/ · repo: https://github.com/Habeeb9731/autonomous-data-analyst\nRole: Design & full-stack development\nTimeline: 2026\nType: Personal project\nStack: React, FastAPI, Cloudflare\nBefore any real analysis, every dataset needs the same groundwork: checking types, missing values, duplicates, outliers and distributions. Autonomous Data Analyst automates that first pass and presents it as a readable workspace — overview, data quality, insights and charts.\nChallenge: AI analysis you can actually trust\n- Language models are good at explaining data but can invent numbers.\n- Messy real-world files: mixed types, inconsistent categories, missing values.\n- Results had to be clear to people who don’t read raw statistics.\nSolution: Deterministic numbers, readable findings\n- A FastAPI profiling engine computes every metric deterministically — the same file always gives the same report.\n- Issues are grouped by severity with the affected columns, so it is obvious what to fix first.\n- Findings and charts are generated from the computed profile, keeping explanations grounded in real values.\nFeatures: Data quality: A canonical quality score with detected issues — missing values, inconsistencies, outliers.; Insights: Evidence-backed findings, each linked to the columns and values behind it.; Charts: Distributions and category breakdowns chosen automatically from the dataset’s structure.\nFrontend: Vite + React + TypeScript workspace, deployed on Cloudflare Pages.\nProfiling engine: Python / FastAPI service computing deterministic dataset profiles.\nProduction API: Deployed as a Cloudflare Python Worker; local development uses FastAPI behind a Vite proxy.\nDemo mode: A built-in demo dataset so visitors can explore without uploading anything.\nStack: React, TypeScript, Vite, Python, FastAPI, Cloudflare Pages, Cloudflare Workers\nWhat he learned: Separating “compute” from “explain” was the key decision. Keeping every number deterministic and only using AI-style summaries on top of it made the tool far more trustworthy.\nCase study: https://abdulhabeeb.com/projects/autonomous-data-analyst/\n\n### EDEN Smart Terrarium (IoT · Group project)\nA smart dual-plant terrarium and its live dashboard — sensors, pumps, a fan and a grow light, built as a group project for the Interactive Systems course.\nLinks: live: https://eden.abdulhabeeb.com/ · repo: https://github.com/Habeeb9731/eden\nRole: Team member · web dashboard\nTimeline: 2026\nType: Group project · Interactive Systems, Saarland University\nStack: ESP32, C++, HTML/CSS/JS\nEDEN is a smart terrarium with two independent watering zones. An ESP32 sensor hub reads soil moisture, temperature, humidity, light and the water reservoir, and drives two pumps, a fan and a grow light. The web dashboard turns those readings into a calm, glanceable view and lets you take over manual control.\nChallenge: Real hardware, real constraints\n- Two plants with different watering needs in the same enclosure.\n- A small sensor hub on a local network, with request timeouts under load.\n- Automation that people can trust and override.\nSolution: Simple, transparent, offline-first\n- Independent per-zone soil moisture and pumps, each with its own thresholds.\n- A dependency-free PWA that polls the hub directly and handles a disconnected device gracefully.\n- Auto / Manual control for every actuator, with plain-language explanations of why automation is acting.\nFeatures: Plant health: A single health score plus humidity, soil and reservoir levels on the home screen.; Systems control: Auto or manual control of Pump A, Pump B, the fan and the grow light.; Works offline: Installable PWA with a demo mode that simulates the terrarium when the device is unreachable.\nHardware: ESP32 sensor hub with soil moisture, temperature, humidity, light and reservoir sensors; pumps, fan and grow light.\nDevice firmware: C++ on the ESP32, plus a touchscreen interface on the device itself.\nDashboard: Vanilla HTML, CSS and JavaScript — zero dependencies, zero CDN calls, installable as a PWA.\nTeam: Built by Team 17 for the Interactive Systems course at Saarland University.\nStack: ESP32, C++, HTML, CSS, JavaScript, PWA, Service worker\nWhat he learned: Building for real hardware changes priorities: graceful failure, clear status and trustworthy automation matter more than features. Working in a team also meant agreeing early on the data contract between the device and the dashboard.\nCase study: https://abdulhabeeb.com/projects/eden-smart-terrarium/\n\n### GrandLense (Web · Photography)\nA portfolio website for a wedding and portrait photography studio — built to let the photographs lead.\nLinks: live: https://grandlense.com · repo: https://github.com/Habeeb9731/grandlense\nRole: Web design & development\nTimeline: 2024\nType: Client project\nStack: HTML, CSS, JavaScript, PHP\nGrandLense is a photography studio covering weddings, portrait sessions and creative shoots. The brief was a portfolio site that feels as cinematic as the work — full-screen imagery, minimal interface, and an easy way for couples to get in touch.\nChallenge: Large images, fast site\n- The site is almost entirely high-resolution photography.\n- Visitors mostly arrive on phones, often from social media.\n- Enquiries needed to reach the studio reliably.\nSolution: Image-first, lightweight build\n- A full-screen slider with bold typography and minimal chrome, so each photo gets the whole screen.\n- Responsive layouts and compressed imagery for fast loading on mobile.\n- A simple PHP contact form that sends enquiries straight to the studio.\nFeatures: Cinematic slider: Full-bleed photography with large headline typography and autoplay controls.; Mobile-first: Layouts tuned for phones, where most visitors browse.; Direct enquiries: A contact form connected to the studio’s inbox.\nFrontend: Hand-built HTML, CSS and JavaScript with a full-screen slider.\nContact: PHP contact form for enquiries.\nHosting: Live at grandlense.com.\nStack: HTML, CSS, JavaScript, PHP\nWhat he learned: For visual businesses, restraint is the design. Most of the work was removing interface so the photography could carry the site.\nCase study: https://abdulhabeeb.com/projects/grandlense/\n\n### Chick Bites Shawarma (Web · Restaurant)\nA responsive website for a café in Surathkal specialising in shawarmas and burgers — menu, story and table reservations.\nLinks: repo: https://github.com/Habeeb9731/food-website\nRole: Web design & development\nTimeline: 2024\nType: Client project\nStack: HTML, SCSS, JavaScript\nChick Bites is a café in Surathkal serving shawarmas and burgers. The goal was a warm, appetising website that shows the menu, tells the café’s story and makes it easy to book a table — especially from a phone.\nChallenge: Small business, big first impression\n- Most customers discover the café on their phones.\n- The menu changes, so it had to be easy to update.\n- Reservations needed a simple, friendly flow.\nSolution: Warm design, simple structure\n- A food-led visual style with large photography and a handwritten logo.\n- Clear menu pages and a straightforward reservation form.\n- Responsive layouts so the site works just as well on mobile.\nFeatures: Menu: Menu pages that put the dishes front and centre.; Reservations: A simple table booking form — party size, date and time.; Mobile-friendly: Built to work smoothly on phones, where most visitors browse.\nFrontend: HTML, SCSS and JavaScript, built from a restaurant theme and customised for the café.\nPages: Home, menu, story, gallery and reservations.\nStack: HTML, SCSS, JavaScript\nWhat he learned: Small-business sites succeed on clarity: what you serve, where you are, how to book. Everything else is secondary.\nCase study: https://abdulhabeeb.com/projects/chick-bites/\n\n### Modern Luxury Interiors (Web · Interiors)\nA polished website for a Dubai interior design firm — modern interiors with a luxury finish, presented with the same care.\nLinks: live: https://modernluxury-design.com/\nRole: Web design & development\nTimeline: 2024\nType: Client project\nStack: HTML, CSS, JavaScript\nModern Luxury is a Dubai-based interior design and fit-out firm. The site presents their services — from architectural design and turnkey projects to landscaping and technical services — with a refined, architectural visual language.\nChallenge: Many services, one premium brand\n- A wide range of services — design, fit-out, landscaping, maintenance.\n- A premium audience that judges quality instantly.\n- Enquiries needed to be quick, including via WhatsApp.\nSolution: Architectural design, clear structure\n- Bold typography over architectural photography to set a premium tone.\n- Dedicated pages for each service so clients find exactly what they need.\n- Prominent quote and WhatsApp contact options on every page.\nFeatures: Service pages: Separate pages for architecture design, turnkey, landscaping and technical services.; Portfolio: Project portfolio and detail pages to showcase completed work.; Fast contact: “Get a quote” and WhatsApp buttons always within reach.\nFrontend: HTML, CSS and JavaScript multi-page site.\nPages: Home, about, services (and service details), portfolio, team, blog and contact.\nHosting: Live at modernluxury-design.com.\nStack: HTML, CSS, JavaScript\nWhat he learned: Premium brands are judged on details — spacing, type and photography choices did more for the brand than any feature.\nCase study: https://abdulhabeeb.com/projects/modern-luxury-interiors/\n\n### Ecliniqs (Web · Healthcare)\nA website for an online speech therapy provider based in Dubai — services for children and adults, plus online appointment booking.\nLinks: repo: https://github.com/Habeeb9731/ecliniqs_deploy\nRole: Web design & development\nTimeline: 2024\nType: Client project\nStack: HTML, CSS, JavaScript, PHP\nEcliniqs offers online speech therapy for children and adults, plus family counselling. Families often arrive worried and unsure where to start, so the site focuses on reassurance: clear services, friendly imagery and an easy way to book a session.\nChallenge: A sensitive, unfamiliar service\n- Many families have never tried online therapy before.\n- Different services for children, adults and families.\n- Booking needed to be simple for non-technical users.\nSolution: Reassuring design, guided booking\n- Warm photography and plain-language copy that explains how sessions work.\n- Separate service pages for child therapy, adult therapy and family counselling.\n- An appointment page with calendar-based booking and a payment page.\nFeatures: Clear services: Dedicated pages for paediatric, adult and family services.; Online booking: Calendar-based appointment scheduling.; Payments: A payment page to confirm sessions online.\nFrontend: HTML, CSS and JavaScript multi-page site.\nBackend: PHP for forms and contact.\nPages: Home, about, child services, adult services, family counselling, appointment, payment and contact.\nStack: HTML, CSS, JavaScript, PHP\nWhat he learned: Designing for healthcare is designing for anxious users. Tone, clarity and a short path to booking mattered more than visual flair.\nCase study: https://abdulhabeeb.com/projects/ecliniqs/\n\n### MemorLoop (UX · HCI project)\nA desktop learning app concept built around interactive flashcards and spaced repetition to improve memory retention.\nLinks: live: https://www.figma.com/proto/dHLZv3MOHBEXeluwoeAuS6/HCI-Final-Project?page-id=0%3A1&node-id=8-2&viewport=-441%2C259%2C0.08&t=w5cBSRvvlj1Ln3ls-1&scaling=min-zoom&content-scaling=fixed&starting-point-node-id=8%3A2&show-proto-sidebar=1\nRole: UX research & UI design\nTimeline: University project\nType: HCI course project\nStack: Figma\nMemorLoop is a desktop learning experience designed in Figma for an HCI course. It combines notes, uploaded study material and interactive flashcards with spaced repetition, so reviews are scheduled around how memory actually fades.\nChallenge: Studying is fragmented\n- Notes, PDFs and flashcards usually live in separate tools.\n- Most learners review too late — or too often.\n- Progress is hard to see, which hurts motivation.\nSolution: One space, smart repetition\n- A single dashboard bringing together uploads, notes and flashcards.\n- Spaced repetition that schedules reviews based on how well each card is remembered.\n- Visible progress charts to keep learners motivated.\nFeatures: Flashcards: Interactive cards created from notes and uploaded material.; Spaced repetition: Reviews scheduled at the moment you’re about to forget.; Progress tracking: Learning progress charts that make improvement visible.\nProcess: User research, task flows and iterative prototyping as part of an HCI course.\nDesign: High-fidelity desktop UI and interactive prototype in Figma.\nStack: Figma, UX research, Prototyping\nWhat he learned: Grounding design decisions in how memory actually works — rather than in features — gave the product a clear core: show the right card at the right time.\nCase study: https://abdulhabeeb.com/projects/memorloop/\n";
